TI  - Using imaging as a biomarker for asthma.
PG  - 1-10
LID - S0091-6749(16)31372-0 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jaci.2016.11.009 [doi]
AB  - There have been significant advancements in the various imaging techniques being 
      used for the evaluation of asthmatic patients, both from a clinical and research 
      perspective. Imaging characteristics can be used to identify specific asthmatic 
      phenotypes and provide a more detailed understanding of endotypes contributing to 
      the pathophysiology of the disease. Computed tomography, magnetic resonance 
      imaging, and positron emission tomography can be used to assess pulmonary 
      structure and function. It has been shown that specific airway and lung density 
      measurements using computed tomography correlate with clinical parameters, 
      including severity of disease and pathology, but also provide unique phenotypes. 
      Hyperpolarized (129)Xe and (3)He are gases used as contrast media for magnetic 
      resonance imaging that provide measurement of distal lung ventilation reflecting 
      small-airway disease. Positron emission tomography can be useful to identify and 
      target lung inflammation in asthmatic patients. Furthermore, imaging techniques 
      can serve as a potential biomarker and be used to assess response to 
      therapies, including newer biological treatments and bronchial thermoplasty.
