TI  - Interfering with airway nerves in cough associated with asthma.
PG  - 101854
LID - S1094-5539(19)30172-5 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.pupt.2019.101854 [doi]
AB  - Cough is a troublesome and often refractory symptom of asthma, which is 
      associated with poor control of disease. The pathogenesis of asthmatic cough has 
      mainly been attributed to bronchoconstriction, but recent evidence indicate that 
      cough reflex hypersensitivity or neuronal dysfunction is a feature of asthma, 
      even in those with mild stable disease. This is likely resistant to the mainstay 
      treatment ICS/LABA which inhibits classic asthmatic response. Such refractory 
      cough might manifest more predominantly in the day-time rather than night-time. 
      Treatment options of such refractory cough or cough reflex hypersensitivity in 
      asthma targeting the nerves (LTRAs, tiotropium, and potentially bronchial 
      thermoplasty) are discussed.
